  <h2>Which should you choose?</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Want a finished product</strong> — Claude Code or Codex, if you're in their ecosystem.</li>
    <li><strong>Want cheap DeepSeek in a minimal tool</strong> — Pi, Reasonix, or CodeWhale.</li>
    <li><strong>Want a framework to build on</strong> — DeepSeek Harness.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>FAQ</h2>
  <h3>Is DSH cheaper than Pi or Reasonix?</h3>
  <p>Out of the box, not necessarily — Pi's minimal design is deliberately tuned for a 99.93% cache hit rate. DSH's minimal and PTC modes aim at the same economics, but DSH trades a bit of that single-mindedness for extensibility. If pure per-task cost is the only metric, Pi is hard to beat.</p>
  <h3>Why does the same DeepSeek model cost 7x more in Claude Code?</h3>
  <p>Claude Code's request shape — larger tool sets, history handling, and prompt structure — isn't built for DeepSeek's prefix caching. The model is identical; the harness re-sends more context on every step.</p>
  <h3>Is the DSH plugin ecosystem ready for production?</h3>
  <p>Early. 101 community plugins are cataloged, but 81 are GitHub-source (not npm), 3 fail to install, and you'll hit build/ESM errors on a few. It's usable if you're willing to be your own quality gate.</p>
  <h3>Which of these is most future-proof?</h3>
  <p>DSH if you believe the ecosystem compounds; Pi or Reasonix if you want a finished minimal tool today. Claude Code and Codex are safe but lock you to their model economics.</p>

